Time out unplaceable KubeRay worker pods sooner than placed ones that are starting.

The autoscaler moves an ALLOCATED instance to ALLOCATION_TIMEOUT once it stays
ALLOCATED for RAY_AUTOSCALER_RECONCILE_ALLOCATE_STATUS_TIMEOUT_S (an hour by
default), whether its pod can't be placed on any node or is placed and still
pulling its image or waiting for its node to boot. With several worker groups
ranked by priority, one timeout can't fit both: a short one drops pods that are
about to start, and a long one waits on a group that has no capacity before
trying the next one.

KubeRayProvider now marks a worker pod that has no node and either keeps
scheduling gates (such as a queue's admission gate) or is reported Unschedulable
by the scheduler, and the reconciler times out such an ALLOCATED instance after
RAY_AUTOSCALER_RECONCILE_UNSCHEDULABLE_TIMEOUT_S (two minutes by default, never
longer than the allocate timeout). A pod bound to a node keeps the allocate
timeout.

A pod the scheduler rejected can also be waiting for a new node that is still
booting or registering its devices. When a Karpenter NodeClaim (karpenter.sh/v1)
that is not being deleted and whose Initialized condition is not True agrees with
the pod's node selector, the provider marks the pod as having a host
provisioning, and the reconciler gives it
RAY_AUTOSCALER_RECONCILE_PROVISIONING_TIMEOUT_S (ten minutes by default, never
longer than the allocate timeout) instead. A failed NodeClaim lookup, such as a
missing CRD or missing permission, leaves the two-minute timeout.

Upstream status: not yet reported to ray-project/ray.

